✅ **F-1620-7 (s1620 2026-08-10, CLOSED s1623, VERIFIED AT THE f1619-2 DRAIN BY READING BOTH SITES — A BUDGET CEILING GUARDED BY TWO DISAGREEING INSTRUMENTS IS A GUARD THAT WILL EVENTUALLY BE BELIEVED AT THE WRONG NUMBER.)** ⚙️ `e2e/asset-diet.spec.ts` asserts `townResponseBytes < 25_000_000` in **two** tests — the pre-existing throttled-cues test at `:103` and the new A/B test at `:225`/`:226`. Both name the quantity `townResponseBytes`. **Their values differ by 9.5 MB on mobile:** the pre-existing test reports **12,932,580** (12,067,420 headroom) while the new normal arm reports **22,469,519** (**2,530,481 headroom**). ➡️ **The project's belief about how much room it has under the ceiling depends on WHICH TEST YOU READ, and the tighter number is the new one — 2.5 MB of real headroom, not 12 MB.** ? **INFERRED, and flagged as inference rather than measured:** the older test throttles GLBs by design, so its total is plausibly a *partial* load captured inside the measurement window — but the drain did NOT verify that, and **the two must not be reconciled by assumption.** ⚠️ **CONSEQUENCE FOR THE QUESTION f1619-2 WAS AUTHORED TO SETTLE:** f1615-1's disputed figures (desktop `21,389,200 → 16,209,181`, mobile `15,181,572 → 17,074,995`) sit **between the two instruments' scales**, so the unverified **~1.9 MB "growth" may itself be partly an instrument mismatch rather than a real regression.** 💡 **This slice therefore does NOT close f1615-1 — it establishes that closing it requires naming the instrument first**, which is a better outcome than a delta number that would only have invited a story. 📈 **STRENGTHENED s1620, MEASURED BY THIS FIRE’S OWN DEPLOY — IT IS THREE INSTRUMENTS, NOT TWO, AND THE THIRD IS THE ONE THAT GATES RELEASES.** `scripts/deploy.sh` runs its own **first-town asset budget** check against the **same 25,000,000 ceiling** — its printed headroom sums to exactly 25,000,000, so the ceiling is identical, not merely similar — and reported **desktop 16,174,837 (8,825,163 headroom) · mobile 15,218,288 (9,781,712 headroom)** on the very build published as `891edb2c3`. ⚠️ **So MOBILE now has three different answers to one question: 12,932,580 (throttled-cues test) · 15,218,288 (deploy gate) · 22,469,519 (the new A/B normal arm) — a spread of 9.5 MB against a 25 MB ceiling.** 🎯 **This is what raises the finding from tidy-up to load-bearing: the deploy figure is the one that can BLOCK A RELEASE, and it is the second-most optimistic of the three.** If the A/B arm is the honest measure of what a traveller actually pulls, the release gate is passing builds with **~2.5 MB** of real headroom while reporting **~9.8 MB**. ℹ️ **Stated as the open question it is, not as a verdict:** the three may legitimately measure different scopes (throttled partial load · built-bundle static budget · full navigated walk), and **this fire did NOT establish which is right** — only that three named instruments answer one named ceiling differently and nothing reconciles them. **GATE: none owed to the owner. Closes when the two `townResponseBytes` sites either measure the same thing or are renamed so they cannot be confused, AND f1615-1's figures are restated against a named instrument. RECOMMEND doing this BEFORE any further asset-diet tuning — a 2.5 MB real headroom is a very different planning constraint from 12 MB.** ✅ **CLOSED s1623 2026-08-10 by `181e1835b` (f1621-1, review `reviews/f1621-1-town-budget-instrument-reconciliation.md`) — BOTH limbs of the gate satisfied.** The ceiling is now one exported `TOWN_TRANSFER_CEILING_BYTES` in place of four bare literals; the two quantities are renamed apart (`cueWindowResponseBytes` vs `normalBytes`) so they can no longer be confused; f1615-1s figures are restated against the **throttled-cues / cue-window instrument**. ⚠️ **But read F-1623-1 before you use ANY number in this row:** every figure quoted above is a single draw from an instrument the drain has since measured as unstable by up to **3.08x at fix