tasks/BACKLOG.md:993:🟡 **F-1383-2 (s1383, MEASURED — "THE CHEAPEST REAL WORK ON THE BOARD" IS NOT A TEST REPAIR, AND IT LEADS STRAIGHT INTO A BLOCKED LEAF. DO NOT AUTHOR IT AS A SCOPED FIXTURE FIX.)** s1380 filed F-1380-2 and called it *"the cheapest real work on the board"*; s1381 repeated that. I verified the subject before authoring a master against it (**a measurement task must verify its subject is present**) and the framing does not survive contact. ✓ **STILL RED, both projects, re-measured s1383 at `--workers=1`** — but **it fails at `e2e/agent-view.spec.ts:266`, the `expect(ids).toEqual([...])` list, NOT at `:267` where the fixture comparison lives.** The live family is **SIX** contracts — `["the-claim","e1-drill-yard","e1-dry-gulch","e1-night-shift","e1-twin-banks","e1-baron"]` — while the test hardcodes **five** and omits `e1-drill-yard`; **the test's own title still says "all five"**, so the title is a stale claim too. ⇒ **The early assertion DISABLES everything after it: `expect(manifests).toEqual(fixtures)` and the byte-stability compare have not executed at all.** Calling this a fixture-drift repair mis-states which assertion is broken and hides that the fixture oracle is **dark**, not failing. 🔗 **AND REPAIRING `:266` WALKS DIRECTLY INTO A BLOCKED LEAF.** `f1328-1-drill-yard-census-debt` (now **`blockClass: "disputed"`** per F-1383-1) is about **exactly this subject**: *"deriveMechanicsManifest(\"e1-drill-yard\") returns interactables:[] and rules river/water_crossings despite the yard declaring a bell, dummies, practice gold and buildables — the derivation ignores `practice`, an AP-11 NO-UNDECLARED-MECHANICS violation AT THE DERIVATION."* Adding `e1-drill-yard` to the id list makes the next two assertions compare that derivation against the fixture — which is precisely the **JUDGEMENT its leaf reserves**: *"does a byte-stable fixture of F-1328-4's wrong derivation bless it or catch it?"* That leaf also records **two dispatches already** and *"THIRD ATTEMPT REQUIRES A CHANGED PREMISE per the twice-then-escalate law."* 🚫 **THEREFORE: NOT fire-authorable as written, and this is why s1383 authored NO refill master despite two USABLE lanes.** Inventing a scoped test repair here would have been the [do-not-touch list contains the blocker] shape — the cheap-looking half is load-bearing on the reserved half. ➡️ **RECOMMENDATION (needs no owner word): the changed premise the leaf asks for is option (a) — fix the DERIVATION in its own slice first** (`practice` declarations must reach `deriveMechanicsManifest`), **then** the id list, title and fixture all follow mechanically and the byte-stable oracle lights up for the first time. That ordering also converts the disputed leaf's judgement question into a moot one, because the fixture would then bless a *correct* derivation. 💡 **The reusable half: when an inherited finding says "cheapest work on the board", re-measure WHICH ASSERTION FAILS before believing the scope. A red attributed to the assertion it is NAMED after, rather than the one that actually threw, will size the job wrong every time — and here it also concealed that a second, more expensive question was sitting one line below it.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1027:🟢 **F-1372-1 (s1372, MEASURED — NEGATIVE RESULT: NO SPEC SLICE IS LOST, BECAUSE SLICE IDS ARE NOT THE LEDGER’S KEY EITHER — AND THE NEW PART IS THAT ABSENCE FROM THE LEDGER IS *BIDIRECTIONAL*, SO THIS ENTIRE CLASS OF SWEEP HAS A SIGN AMBIGUITY AT ITS CORE.)** 🧭 **THE PROBE.** s1370 §K(3) offered two surfaces as *hypotheses, not work*; s1371 took the first (`reviews/` F-ids) and deliberately left the second. This is the second and last: **`specs/**` slices whose playable checkpoints no goal leaf claims.** Per s1371 §K(3) I derived what enumerates the set before counting it, and per s1371 §H(3) **every absence-grep in this fire ran UNCHAINED (`;`, never `&&`)**. 📐 **THE DENOMINATOR HAD TO BE DERIVED, AND THE DOCUMENT-CLASS TRAP s1371 PREDICTED IS REAL.** `specs/` = **77 files / 41 directories** and is **not one class**: RATIFIED specs · DRAFTs · **SEED / “DISCUSSION STATUS, NOT BUILDABLE”** docs (`token-connection`, `loot-and-builds`, `contract-editor`) · a **SUPERSEDED** spec (`outlaw-intelligence`) · **content bundles** (`epoch-saga/e3..e10`) · **recipes** (`town-3d/RECIPE.md`) · **file-per-slice dirs** (`m0`/`m1`/`m2`/`visual-polish`, where the ID is the *filename*). Slice IDs are uppercase tokens declared as `- **RF-01 — …**` or `SLICE PC-01:`. Extracting from declaration-shaped lines only: **133 candidates**, of which I dropped **5 measured false positives** (`F-1`..`F-5` in `e10-static-mechanic-DRAFT.md` are **finding ids inside a spec** — verified by reading `:265-269`) and **17 BUILD-PLAN per-epoch work-class templates** (`ENG-1`/`CON-1`/`ART-1`/`CER-1` recur for every epoch E2–E10; they are a *plan* vocabulary, not one spec’s slices) ⇒ **111 slice IDs**. ⚖️ **THE STRUCTURAL RESULT, AND IT SETTLES THE PREMISE BEFORE THE RESIDUE MATTERS: 60 in `goals.json` · 95 in `BACKLOG` · 60 in BOTH · 0 in goals-only.** **`goals.json` is a STRICT SUBSET of BACKLOG.** So the question as posed — *“no goal leaf claims it”* — would have reported **51 misses**, and **35 of those are lawfully tracked BACKLOG rungs**. Asking the goal tree is asking the wrong index, exactly as F-ids were the wrong index in F-1371-1. ✅ **THE 16 IN NEITHER, ALL READ AT SOURCE, NOT SAMPLED — ALL 16 LAWFUL, IN FOUR DISTINCT WAYS.** **(1) `ST-00`..`ST-07` (8 — the largest block, and the one that looked like a real finding):** `specs/e10-static-mechanic-DRAFT.md` is headed **RATIFIED 2026-08-01** by the owner *(“(Q1) option (a) (Q2) option (a) (Q3) option (a)”)*, so 8 slices of an owner-ratified spec appear in no ledger. **Innocent, and already known:** the ruling’s scope clause is ***post-launch*** — recorded at `BACKLOG:2406` and `specs/e10-static-mechanic-DRAFT.md:3` — and **F-1363-1 (s1363) already found the one surface that had lost the qualifier and cured it in `f1308ee9` with an explicit DO-NOT-AUTHOR.** Post-launch is unsatisfied today (`git tag` carries no version tag). **(2) `W1-02`, `W1-06` (2): SHIPPED.** `reviews/w1-02-living-water.md` and `reviews/w1-06-vista.md` exist and `vista` is live in **6 `src/` files**; their ladder rows were retired per §4. **(3) `PB-02` (1): SHIPPED at `5a153e95`** — *“✅ PB-01+02 … EVENT PARITY PROVEN”* (`BACKLOG:1358`). **(4) `MP-05`/`MU-01`/`SS-04`/`ED-06`/`ED-07` (5): owner-gated or not-yet-ratified by construction** — MP-05 is *“owner + son, two devices”*, an acceptance **playtest** (co-op is a PARKED named milestone, `BACKLOG:1408`); MU-01 ends *“→ owner picks”*; SS-04 is a DRAFT rung explicitly sequenced *“With/after the Baron task”*; ED-06/07 sit in a **SEED** spec carrying the owner’s own unanswered question (*“Is it overkill? Should we use something existing?”*), ED-07 marked **OWNER-ONLY v1**. ➡️ **THE STRUCTURAL ANSWER, THIRD ROAD TO THE SAME PLACE: THE LEDGER INDEXES WORK BY THREAD AND RUNG NAME, NOT BY EVERY ID TOKEN A SPEC MINTS.** The e10 Static thread is in BACKLOG **48 times** — as *“e10-static-boss”*, *“roster-wiring-e6-e10”*, *“THE STATIC”* — and **never once as `ST-04`**. A set-difference on slice IDs measures **namespace mismatch**, precisely as F-1371-1 found for F-ids. ⚠️ **TWO INSTRUMENT DEFECTS OF MY OWN, BOTH CAUGHT BEFORE THE COUNT REACHED THE HANDOFF.** **(1) COMPOUND RUNG NOTATION IS INVISIBLE TO A PER-ID WORD-BOUNDARY TEST.** BACKLOG ships PB-02 as **`PB-01+02`**, which `\bPB-02\b` cannot match — I read it as unindexed when it is *shipped and gated*. Swept the class: exactly **2 members** repo-wide (`PB-01+02`, `TP-01+02`). **(2) MY `isDecl` HEURISTIC COUNTED A MENTION AS A DECLARATION** — `W1-02` was harvested from a bullet in `e5-deepwater-bundle.md` describing *“the W1-02 water shader, grown up”*. 🚫 **RECOMMENDATION: NOTHING TO BUILD — AND DO NOT BUILD A specs-vs-BACKLOG SLICE-ID GUARD.** Three independent reasons, the third of which is new and is the real contribution: **(a)** it would red **16 rows** against a ledger measured clean; **(b)** its denominator must encode a **seven-way document-class distinction that exists nowhere in the filesystem** — status is a line, never a filename, by house convention; **(c) ⭐ ABSENCE FROM THE LEDGER IS BIDIRECTIONAL.** §4 requires finished rungs to be **retired** from the ladder, so *“absent from BACKLOG”* is equally the signature of **never-started** work and of **completed-and-retired** work — **3 of my 16 (19%) were the latter.** A guard firing on absence cannot tell the two apart, so **it would report finished work as missing** — the false-NOT-DONE shape F-1368-1 named, mechanised and put on a 5-minute cadence. 💡 **THE THROUGH-LINE, AND IT CLOSES THE SET RATHER THAN EXTENDING IT.** s1369: your freshly-built instrument is the most dangerous artifact on a dry board. s1370: the instrument can be right and the inference wrong. s1371: the result can be fabricated because the probe never ran. **s1372: THE INSTRUMENT, THE INFERENCE AND THE PROBE CAN ALL BE SOUND — AND THE *QUESTION* STILL WRONG, BECAUSE IT ASSUMED A KEY THE LEDGER DOES NOT USE.** Both surfaces s1370 offered have now been swept and both dissolved the same way, which is itself the finding: **this factory’s ledger is organised by THREAD, and every audit that joins on a minted ID — F-ids, slice IDs — will keep rediscovering that fact at the cost of a fire.** ➡️ **The rule I’d hand forward: before measuring what an index is missing, verify that the thing you are joining on is the index’s KEY. Ask what the ledger is organised BY, not what tokens appear in it.** ⓘ And the honest footnote: the ST-00..ST-07 block looked like the best finding available to this fire — 8 slices, owner-ratified *yesterday*, in no ledger — and it took one `grep` of the defence to dissolve it. **s1370’s rule (“ask what would make this innocent, and go read THAT”) is what turned a dramatic false finding into a paragraph.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1117:✅ **F-1302-1 (s1302, MEASURED THIS FIRE — THE STANDING `066-walk8-engine` KNOWN-RED IS FOUR STALE ASSERTIONS, NOT THE ONE LINE THE LEDGER ADVERTISED; AND THE BOARD HELD *TWO* WRONG LABELS FOR IT, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS).** Two consecutive handoffs (s1300 §F(5), s1301 §F(3)) carried *"`066-walk8-engine`'s half-done rename still decays into a standing known-red"* without action, so I took it. ✓ **MEASURED, NOT INHERITED, AND AT `--workers=1` SO IT IS NOT THE FIRE SHELL (§3.1/F-1270-1):** `:234` expects `char-jumper-sheet-walk8-`, receives **`char-bandit-base-sheet-walk8-r2c0.png`**. 🔑 **THE HALF-DONE RENAME IS *INSIDE ONE TEST*, WHICH IS WHY IT READ AS ONE LINE:** an earlier repair migrated the lookups — `:220` and `:222` already query `char.bandit_base` — **but stopped before the assertions underneath them.** ⚠️ **SO I CORRECTED ONLY THE STRING AND RAN IT AGAIN, WHICH IS THE WHOLE FINDING:** three more assertions, which have **not executed since 2026-07-12**, then red. `:235` `jumper.fps` **7.60** vs expected 8.55 · `:236` `fastJumper.fps` **14.0488** vs 17.1 · `:237` `jumper.stride` **2.8421** vs hero **3.0750**. ✅ `:238` `fastJumper.stride` **3.0750 == 3.0750** — already correct, to four decimals, and it must not be touched. Probe reverted by `git checkout`; `git status --porcelain -- e2e/` **EMPTY** before I authored anything. 🧮 **RECONCILED AGAINST SOURCE, SO THE ENGINE IS PROVABLY NOT ON TRIAL — every number falls out of `Balance`:** `pools.ts:236` hands the animator `speed = groundSpeed*4/(strideUnits*visualScale*walkFpsPerSpeed)`, and `SpriteAnimator` computes `fps = max(walkMinFps, speed*walkFpsPerSpeed) * cadenceFrameScale` (`:590-607`, `:1091-1094`; scale `= 8/4 = 2`). **Compose them and the speed terms cancel: stride-per-cycle is pinned to `strideUnits*visualScale` = `2.05*1.5` = `3.075` — the identical product s1181 derived independently for the hero.** That is why `:238` passes exactly. **Except below the floor:** `walkMinFps = 9.5*0.4 = 3.8`, floored fps `= 3.8*2 =` **7.60 — precisely the slow-arm measurement**, and the knee sits at `groundSpeed = 7.6*3.075/8 ≈ **2.921**` while the test scripts its slow arm at **2.7** (`:215`), *just* under it. ➡️ **THEREFORE `:237` IS NOT MIS-NUMBERED, IT IS UNSATISFIABLE AS WRITTEN**: below the knee the engine *deliberately* suspends stride preservation to stop a crawling walker animating at an ugly rate. Re-baselining it to `2.8421` would convert a law into a rubber stamp — the cure is to assert the **floor** there and keep the **invariant** assert on the fast arm, where it holds exactly. 🏷️ **BOTH LABELS ON THE BOARD WERE WRONG, AND THE COSTLIER ONE WAS THE CAUTIOUS ONE.** F-1294-2 (L344) called it *"fire-authorable as a scoped test repair"* — **too small by 4×**, and a runner given that scope would have hit three unexplained reds with no license to stop. Meanwhile the predecessor leaf `066-walk8-hero-expectation-realign`'s own note in `tasks/goals.json` called `:234` *"the owner-gated F-1166-1 jumper sheet-family fork"* — **too large, and that is the one that froze it for 19 days.** ✓ **REFUTED AT SOURCE:** `:234` names the sheet of `char.bandit_base`, the slot the runtime actually mounts, and F-1166-1's fork is about **8-way rotation + `grab`/`flee` clips** for a spec file that does not exist; **both** of its arms leave `char-bandit-base-sheet-*` alone, so the string is **fork-independent**. *An owner-gate label is load-bearing: it stops every fire that reads it, so mis-scoping one costs more than mis-scoping a task.* ✍️ **AUTHORED + QUEUED `lane-b-066-jumper-cadence-realign` → lane-b** (`lane-usable.mjs` **USABLE**, `ahead=0 paths=0 dirt=0`; goal leaf `066-jumper-cadence-realign` registered in the SAME commit, 496→**497** leaves). Scope: string repair · fps expectations **derived from `Balance` instead of hardcoded** (so the next balance change updates them rather than reddening them) · `:237` re-aimed at the floor · `:238` byte-frozen · **all `src/**` firewalled with a STOP instruction**. ⓘ **Report-only rider, and I am stating it as INERT rather than as a bug because I counted the consumers:** `SpriteAnimator.ts:623-628` `walkSpeedForSlot()` still names `charClaimJumper`/`charBaron` and **never** the bandit slots — the same 2026-07-12 rename stranded a second time, in `src/` this time. But `pools.ts:836/838/857` pass a finite `speed` every frame and `:624` short-circuits on `walkCadenceSpeed` **before** reaching the slot list, so the stale branch is unreachable on the mounted path. It is vestigial, in the exact sense F-1144-1 named the `GeneratedClaimJumperThief…` batch names — and vestiges are what hid this class for 15 days the first time. 💡 *Reusable shape: **a stale assertion hides the assertions behind it.** Curing one and re-running is not a formality — it is the only way to see what has not executed since the rename, and here it turned a "one-line repair" into four.* ✅ **SHIPPED s1303 at `b3664210`** (`reviews/066-jumper-cadence-realign.md`) — **the known-red is CLOSED; `066-walk8-engine` is green for the first time since 2026-07-12, 6/6 both projects at `--workers=1`.** ⭐ **THE SLICE CAME BACK BETTER THAN THE MASTER THAT ORDERED IT, AND THAT IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING:** the master said *re-baseline to the measured numbers*; the runner instead **derived** them at runtime from `Balance.anim` + `RUN_CAST_SCALE` (`cadenceSpeed`/`slowExpectedFps`/`fastExpectedFps` computed in-test), so the assertions now **track the source of truth instead of freezing one fire's measurement** — a future balance change will move the expectation with it rather than manufacturing the next stale red. Tolerance also **tightened from 1–2 decimals to 5**. `:237` re-aimed at the floor with the ≈2.921 knee named in a comment; `:238` **byte-unchanged**. Arithmetic re-derived a third time at merge (7.6000 / 14.0487805) and matches s1302 exactly. **Zero `src/`** — `git status --porcelain -- src assets` EMPTY. *The generalisable bit: a master that names the RIGHT SUBJECT can be improved on by the runner; had it hardcoded the numbers as ordered, this row would be a future stale-assertion finding instead of a closure.*
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1118:🔺 **F-1302-2 (s1302, MEASURED — AN OWNER-DESK FORK IS DESCRIBING A CONTRACT THAT NO LONGER EXISTS; HALF OF ITS ARM (b) SHIPPED SIX DAYS AGO AND THE DESK WAS NEVER TOLD).** While proving F-1302-1 fork-independent I had to read what F-1166-1 actually asserts, and two of its load-bearing facts have **rotted**. F-1166-1 (s1166) tells the owner, verbatim: *"both bandit slots' `walk8.grid.rowDirections` is `['s','w','e','n']` with `aliases {se:'e', ne:'e', sw:'w', nw:'w'}` — **the diagonals collapse onto the pure side rows**"*, and *"**the two slots that ARE mounted carry no 8-way art** and no action clips"*. ✓ **RE-MEASURED AT SOURCE THIS FIRE** (`assets/layer-contracts/characters.v2.json`, both mounted slots): `walk8.aliases` is **`{}` — EMPTY**, and `walk8.directions` carries **`sw/se/nw/ne`, all four winds, on `char.bandit_base` AND `char.bandit_thief`**. The diagonals do **not** collapse; the mounted slots **do** carry 8-way art. 🔑 **WHAT CHANGED, AND IT IS ALREADY IN THIS LEDGER:** `lane-c-eight-winds-wiring-enemies` (s1183, L2126) *"binds the three outlaw slots — `char.bandit_base`, `char.bandit_thief`, `char.baron` — to their already-landed `walkdiag8` sheets, and settles the `aliases`"*. **That is half of F-1166-1's arm (b) — "commission real 8-way art for `char.bandit_base`/`_thief` and wire it" — already SHIPPED**, six days after the fork was written, by a slice that had no idea it was answering an owner question. ⚠️ **SO THE FORK AS WRITTEN IS NO LONGER A CHOICE BETWEEN TWO LIVE OPTIONS:** arm **(a) "ACCEPT COARSE"** describes *"4-row walk8 with aliased diagonals"* — **a state that no longer exists**, so accepting it would mean accepting something already superseded; arm **(b)** is **partly done**, and its genuine remainder is now much smaller than the fork implies. ✓ **WHAT IS STILL TRUE, STATED SO THE NEXT READER DOES NOT OVER-CORRECT ME:** `char.bandit_thief` top-level keys are still **exactly `slot`, `fallback`, `walk8`** — **no `clips` block on either mounted slot**, so `grab`/`flee` action art is genuinely still absent and the animator can still only fall back, exactly as F-1166-1 says. `src/entities/Enemy.ts` still sets `spriteClip='grab'`/`'flee'` with no art to serve it. ➡️ **THE HONEST RESIDUAL FORK IS THEREFORE NARROWER AND CHEAPER: the only open question is the `grab`/`flee` ACTION CLIPS, not 8-way walk orientation.** Recommendation unchanged in spirit but re-aimed: **accept the shipped 8-way walk as the answer to the orientation half** (it is live, wired and gated), and rule separately — and later — on whether outlaws get `grab`/`flee` art at all. ⛔ **I did not touch `char.claim_jumper`, `tasks/025` stays DO-NOT-QUEUE, and I am NOT choosing between the arms** — §2E bars that and this line does not pretend otherwise; it repairs the *premise* the owner would rule on. 💡 *Reusable shape: **a desk item ages against a moving tree.** F-1166-1 was measured carefully and was true the day it was written; six days of unrelated shipping falsified two of its facts, and nothing in the factory re-checks a parked question. Before spending an owner action, re-measure the sentence you are asking them to rule on — the cheapest ruling is the one that turns out to be unnecessary.* — ✓ **RE-MEASURED s1342 (currency check): ALL FOUR SUB-CLAIMS STILL TRUE, the residual fork is intact, and no owner action has been overtaken.** `assets/layer-contracts/characters.v2.json` read directly: `char.bandit_base` (`:156–175`) and `char.bandit_thief` (`:176–195`) both enumerate top-level keys **exactly `["slot","fallback","walk8"]`** — `hasOwnProperty('clips')` **false on both**, so grab/flee action art is genuinely still absent; `walk8.aliases` still **`{}`** (`:173`, `:193`); `walk8.directions` still carries **`["sw","se","nw","ne"]`** on both (`:167–172`, `:187–192`). `src/entities/Enemy.ts` still sets `spriteClip='flee'` (`:888`, `:904`) and `'grab'` (`:894`, `:932`, `:985`, `:1024`, `:1059`) with no art to serve them. 🔑 **ONE NUANCE WORTH CARRYING, BECAUSE IT IS THE EXACT AMBIGUITY THAT ROTTED F-1166-1's WORDING IN THE FIRST PLACE:** `walk8.grid.rowDirections` **is still `["s","w","e","n"]`** (`:166`, `:186`) — the 4-row base sheet — and that is **not** a contradiction of the 8-wind claim. The diagonals are not aliased onto those four rows; they come from **separate per-direction `walkdiag8` file lists with their own 8-frame/16fps clips.** So *"4 rows"* and *"8 winds"* are **both true, of different keys**. A future reader checking only `rowDirections` will conclude the diagonals collapse and re-open a fork that is already answered — **read `directions` and `aliases`, not `grid.rowDirections`.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1119:🟡 **F-1303-1 (s1303, MEASURED — ⚠️ DOWNGRADED s1305, THE CHARACTERISATION BELOW IS SUPERSEDED: re-measured in the identical full-file both-project shape, **4 runs / 16 of 16 instances GREEN** where s1303 saw 7 of 12 fail. The reds were real; "order-dependent with a named `:19`→`:39` suspect" is **not supported** — it is a stochastic flake of unknown rate. **Its GATE is replaced by: measure the rate across ≥2 sessions before naming a mechanism or authoring a corrective, and do NOT queue the leakage investigation on this premise.** See **F-1305-3**, which found the same over-claim in F-1304-1 and treats it as a method defect rather than two unlucky rows.) — `eight-winds-enemies.spec.ts:39` IS AN ORDER-DEPENDENT FAILURE, NOT THE LOAD FLAKE I ALMOST FILED IT AS).** Surfaced during the `066-jumper-cadence-realign` adjacent battery: `[mobile-chrome] › eight-winds-enemies.spec.ts:39 › diagnostics drive thief northeast and Baron southwest on their correct rows` died on `page.waitForFunction: Test timeout of 60000ms exceeded`. ✓ **NOT THE DRAINED SLICE, PROVEN NOT ASSUMED:** that merge contains **zero `src/`**, never touches this spec, and this spec does not import the 066 spec — it is not even *collected* in a run of it. The two census commits that landed on main immediately prior (`1078564b`, `ee1d050f`) also touched zero `src`/`e2e` (`git show --stat … -- src e2e` returned **empty**). The spec has existed since `29396f9c` (s1183) and appears in **no** `STATUS.md` / `BACKLOG` / review row as a known red — it is undocumented, not inherited. ⚠️ **THE MEASUREMENT IS THE ROW.** Solo (`-g "diagnostics drive thief"`, `--repeat-each=3`, mobile): **3/3 PASS in 23.9s**. In file order, three separate full-file runs: **mobile 0/3, desktop 1/3**. Every arm at `--workers=1`, so §3.1/F-1270-1 shell-serialisation is **excluded by construction**. When it passes it passes in **~8s**; when it fails it burns the **full 60s** timeout — bimodal, not a slow-machine tail. ➡️ **THEREFORE THE HYPOTHESIS IS STATE LEAKAGE FROM `:19` INTO `:39` IN THE SAME WORKER**, not load: `:19` is a plain boot, `:39` boots `/?debug&contract=e1-baron&nowaves&nolevel&nopause&nokill`. 🔑 **The near-miss is the lesson: the 3/3 solo green is exactly the evidence that would have justified writing "flaky, ignore" — an isolated reproduction is a sample of one, and here the isolation *was itself the variable*.** The discriminating run cost 5 minutes and converted a shrug into a reproducible fault with a named suspect. **GATE: a scoped investigation of `:19`→`:39` leakage — reproduce at `--repeat-each=3` in file order, then bisect by running `:39` after a bare `:19` boot.** Fire-authorable, but size it **after** the first measurement, not before: F-1302-1 is the standing counter-example of an "obviously small" test repair that was four assertions. Not queued this fire (drain budget spent; no master authored). 💡 *Reusable shape: **when a test passes alone and fails in company, the isolation is not a control — it is a second experiment.** "Flaky" is the label we reach for when we have run the test twice the same way; running it two DIFFERENT ways is what turns it into a bug report.*
